WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.663
Here is a line from our lab app, hello-devops.

2
00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:11.588
Redis, the small database that keeps its visit count, is down, so the app writes this error.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.429
All the logs go to one store you can search.

4
00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:17.296
So you type ConnectionError into the search box.

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.917
The word is right there on your screen.

6
00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:21.652
And the answer is zero hits.

7
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:25.583
How can a search miss a word you can read with your own eyes?

8
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:27.106
Hold on to that question.

9
00:00:27.106 --> 00:00:29.908
By the end, that zero will make perfect sense.

10
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:32.561
Let's go back to the hospital.

11
00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:35.850
Every note from every bed is copied to one chart room.

12
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.348
Here are two notes from the payment desk.

13
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:41.177
A doctor asks: which notes say declined?

14
00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:45.940
The simplest plan is to read every note, from the first word to the last.

15
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:49.064
That is what the terminal command grep does.

16
00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:51.172
It reads every word, every time.

17
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.489
Found it.

18
00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:57.460
Then she asks a second question, and the reading starts again from page one.

19
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:58.925
Two notes are fine.

20
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:03.163
But a week of logs from fifty containers is a mountain of notes.

21
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:08.664
By the time the doctor finishes reading, the patient has gone home, and so has the doctor.

22
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:10.265
So here is the wish.

23
00:01:10.265 --> 00:01:15.117
Wouldn't it be nice to do the reading once, when a note arrives, and never again?

24
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:18.571
You already own a thing that grants that wish.

25
00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:20.405
The index at the back of a book.

26
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:23.213
To find declined, you don't read the book.

27
00:01:23.213 --> 00:01:26.407
You look up one word, and it hands you page numbers.

28
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:30.203
Somebody did the slow reading once, when the book was made.

29
00:01:30.203 --> 00:01:32.684
Every reader after that gets it for free.

30
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.541
So the chart room hires a clerk.

31
00:01:35.541 --> 00:01:41.535
Each time a note arrives, the clerk reads it once, and files every word on its own index card.

32
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:42.878
Watch two habits.

33
00:01:42.878 --> 00:01:47.326
It splits the text into words, and it writes each one in lower case.

34
00:01:47.326 --> 00:01:49.680
Capital FAILED becomes plain failed.

35
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.275
On every card, it writes the number of the note the word came from.

36
00:01:54.275 --> 00:01:54.863
Note one.

37
00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:57.025
Note two arrives.

38
00:01:57.025 --> 00:01:58.660
New words get new cards.

39
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.543
Words the clerk has seen before just get one more number.

40
00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:05.973
Payment, for, and order now say: notes one and two.

41
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.559
Now let's search.

42
00:02:07.559 --> 00:02:08.198
Payment.

43
00:02:08.198 --> 00:02:10.196
How many notes come back?

44
00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.475
Pause and guess.

45
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:14.952
Two.

46
00:02:14.952 --> 00:02:20.047
The search went straight to one card, read the numbers one and two, and was done.

47
00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:22.036
Notice what it did not do.

48
00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:24.077
It did not read a single note.

49
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.563
One small change.

50
00:02:25.563 --> 00:02:27.867
Payments, with an s at the end.

51
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:29.056
Pause and guess.

52
00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:32.601
Zero.

53
00:02:32.601 --> 00:02:35.009
There is no card that says payments.

54
00:02:35.009 --> 00:02:38.420
The clerk files words as they are, only lowercased.

55
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.296
It never trims them down to a shorter stem.

56
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.000
Now, our mystery.

57
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.984
Most of us picture search like grep, only faster: it finds any piece of text.

58
00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:49.925
Let's be fair to that picture.

59
00:02:49.925 --> 00:02:53.873
Grep really would find ConnectionError inside the error line.

60
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.532
But Elasticsearch never looks at the notes when you search.

61
00:02:57.532 --> 00:02:58.812
It looks at the cards.

62
00:02:58.812 --> 00:03:01.139
So let's watch the clerk file the error.

63
00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:02.775
Here is the surprise.

64
00:03:02.775 --> 00:03:05.525
Dots between letters do not split a word.

65
00:03:05.525 --> 00:03:08.811
So the whole dotted name goes on one single card.

66
00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:12.304
Service, not, and known each get a card of their own.

67
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:14.333
Now search ConnectionError.

68
00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:17.332
Is there a card with exactly that word on it?

69
00:03:17.332 --> 00:03:18.399
Pause and guess.

70
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:21.818
No.

71
00:03:21.818 --> 00:03:27.913
There is a card for the whole dotted name, but none for connection error on its own.

72
00:03:27.913 --> 00:03:28.639
Zero hits.

73
00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:29.205
Well.

74
00:03:29.205 --> 00:03:34.281
The search swears it has never heard of a word that is sitting right there.

75
00:03:34.281 --> 00:03:35.567
And it isn't lying.

76
00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:39.155
Zero hits really means: no card with that exact word.

77
00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:42.113
So ask for words that really are on cards.

78
00:03:42.113 --> 00:03:45.382
The phrase service not known, from the same error.

79
00:03:45.382 --> 00:03:46.363
Found, at once.

80
00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:48.565
That's the zero from the start.

81
00:03:48.565 --> 00:03:49.789
The error happened.

82
00:03:49.789 --> 00:03:52.432
We asked for a word that was never filed.

83
00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:56.018
Now we can give the real names.

84
00:03:56.018 --> 00:03:59.329
In Elasticsearch, each log event is a document.

85
00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:01.784
The clerk is called the analyzer.

86
00:04:01.784 --> 00:04:03.899
Each word on a card is a token.

87
00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:10.273
And the box of cards is an inverted index: a table from each word to the documents that contain it.

88
00:04:10.273 --> 00:04:14.887
It is the book's index, turned around from the way the notes were written.

89
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.748
Three habits before you trust an empty result.

90
00:04:18.748 --> 00:04:20.139
First, ask the clerk.

91
00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:26.301
Elasticsearch has an analyze request that shows the exact tokens a line would be filed under.

92
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:29.334
Second, not every field gets cut into words.

93
00:04:29.334 --> 00:04:33.472
A keyword field, such as level, is filed whole, capitals and all.

94
00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:38.820
Its stored value is ERROR, so an exact search for error in lower case finds nothing.

95
00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:43.546
Third, a note that arrived this very instant may not be on the cards yet.

96
00:04:43.546 --> 00:04:49.543
Elasticsearch makes new documents searchable within about one second, in a step called a refresh.

97
00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:52.480
So read the whole story out loud.

98
00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.553
The clerk reads each note once, when it arrives.

99
00:04:55.553 --> 00:04:58.690
It files every word, lowercased, on its own card.

100
00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.276
And a search looks up whole cards, never pieces of them.

101
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.604
Search finds whole tokens, never pieces of them.

102
00:05:05.604 --> 00:05:07.932
Ask for words that are really there.

103
00:05:08.133 --> 00:05:09.203
A quick check.

104
00:05:09.203 --> 00:05:11.801
A log line says disk.full.warning.

105
00:05:11.801 --> 00:05:14.169
Does a search for full find it?

106
00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:15.392
Pause and think.

107
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:18.797
No.

108
00:05:18.797 --> 00:05:22.334
Dots between letters keep the whole name as one token.

109
00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:26.919
Search for the whole name, and check with analyze if you are not sure.

110
00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:30.395
Next, many copies of our app will share this one store.

111
00:05:30.395 --> 00:05:32.885
How do we know which copy wrote each line?
